#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171718 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171919 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "308a8c68-628a-522f-9545-17a7165aefdf" :knowledge-point/title "Finite Vs. Infinite Sets"}] :question/id #uuid "23214c12-0d2f-54f1-8d27-d6a70406ff58" :question/math-academy-id "e-13173" :question/problem "Do the sets below have the same cardinality, and why?\n$A=\\{4n \\mid \\: n \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\}, \\qquad B=\\{2, 4, \\ldots, 20 \\}$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinalities of two sets cannot be equal if one set is finite and the other is infinite.\n\nWith that in mind, notice that\n\n- $A=\\{0, \\pm 4, \\pm 8, \\ldots \\}$ is infinite, while\n- $B$ is finite with $\\mid B \\mid = 10$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}A & \\neq & B\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude the following:\n\n> Since $A$ is infinite and $B$ is finite, we have that $\\begin{vmatrix}A & \\neq & B\\end{vmatrix}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171719 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171920 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "13fe2373-2094-5193-8e37-541d3a27e5e1" :knowledge-point/title "Using Arithmetic Sequences to Construct a Bijection"}] :question/id #uuid "1f394047-302d-561b-bae6-b88a881d68f7" :question/math-academy-id "e-13180" :question/problem "Do the following sets have the same cardinality? If so, why?\n\n$A= \\{-7, -2, 3, 8, \\ldots \\}, \\qquad B = \\mathbb{N}$" :question/worked-solution "Two sets $A$ and $B$ have the *same cardinality* if there exists a bijection between $A$ and $B$.\n\nNotice that the elements of $A$ form an (infinite) arithmetic sequence with a common difference $d=5{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= 5 \\\\\n&=-2 - (-7) \\\\\n&= 3 - (-2) \\\\\n&= ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our arithmetic sequence, denoted $a_n$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= -7 + (n - 1) \\cdot 5 \\\\\n&=-7 + 5n - 5 \\\\\n&= 5n - 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as\n\n$$\nA = \\left\\{5n-12 \\mid \\: n \\in \\mathbb{N} \\right\\}\n$$\n\nNow, consider the function $f: B \\to A$ that maps the underlying sets as follows:\n\n| $B$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $\\cdots$ | $n$ | $\\cdots$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $A$ | $-7$ | $-2$ | $3$ | $8$ | $\\cdots$ | $5n-12$ | $\\cdots$ |\n\nIn general, for each $n \\in B$, we have\n\n$$\nf(n) = 5n-12 \\in A\n$$\n\nNotice that $f$ is described in such a way that it is bijective. Indeed, it's\n\n- injective since $f(n) \\neq f(m)$ whenever $n \\neq m$, and\n- surjective since each element in $A$ (the second row) has a pre-image in $B$ (the first row).\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}A & = & B\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\ni.e., the sets have the same cardinality."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171744 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171921 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0445833-499f-577f-8ca7-265f72d24b77" :knowledge-point/title "Using Geometric Sequences to Construct a Bijection"}] :question/id #uuid "5f47fd84-fda1-5eba-b32e-53b07a391b3d" :question/math-academy-id "e-14748" :question/problem "Do the following sets have the same cardinality? If so, why?\n$A = \\mathbb{N}, \\qquad B = \\left\\{1,\\, \\dfrac12,\\, \\dfrac14,\\, \\dfrac18,\\, \\ldots \\right \\}$" :question/worked-solution "Two sets $A$ and $B$ have the *same cardinality* if there exists a bijection between $A$ and $B$.\n\nNotice that the elements of $B$ form an (infinite) geometric sequence with a common ratio\n\n$$\nr=\\dfrac12{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr &= \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{1}{2}))/(1) \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{1}{4}))/((\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\\n&= ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted $a_n$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= 1 \\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{1}{2})^{n - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $B$ in set-builder notation as\n\n$$\nB = \\left\\{\\left(\\dfrac12\\right)^{n-1} \\, \\Bigg \\mid \\: n \\in \\mathbb{N} \\right\\}\n$$\n\nNow, consider the function $f: A \\to B$ that maps the underlying sets as follows:\n\n| $A$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $\\cdots$ | $n$ | $\\cdots$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $B$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac14$ | $\\dfrac18$ | $\\cdots$ | $\\left(\\dfrac12\\right)^{n-1}$ | $\\cdots$ |\n\nIn general, for each $n \\in A$, we have\n\n$$\nf(n) = \\left(\\dfrac12\\right)^{n-1} \\in B\n$$\n\nNotice that $f$ is described in such a way that it is bijective. Indeed, it's\n\n- injective since $f(n) \\neq f(m)$ whenever $n \\neq m$, and\n- surjective since each element in $B$ (the second row) has a pre-image in $A$ (the first row).\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}A & = & B\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\ni.e., the sets have the same cardinality."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172390 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171921 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0445833-499f-577f-8ca7-265f72d24b77" :knowledge-point/title "Using Geometric Sequences to Construct a Bijection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a0e7484-6ac1-56e7-a6bc-8460aad4cc2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 \\cdot 3^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169209} {:answer/id #uuid "95c2a9b6-5787-559f-994b-1c06ca56b42e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3n + 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169210} {:answer/id #uuid "fb039d8e-59e9-57d3-ac70-f1887d0e89a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169211} {:answer/id #uuid "fd19cfe1-e41a-5841-a90d-f9871d55f368" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 \\cdot 2^{n - 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169212} {:answer/id #uuid "783301a1-6358-5a0b-9ce7-a229395f0f36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db